{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>וְהוֹצֵאתִי, וְהִצַּלְתִּי, וְגָאַלְתִּי, וְלָקַחְתִּי.</b> Corresponding to four things that befell them, as it is said (Genesis 15:13) ״כִּי גֵר יִהְיֶה זַרְעֲךָ״ — behold, the ger-ship, the strangerhood. ״בְּאֶרֶץ לֹא לָהֶם״ — behold, the withdrawal of the Shekhinah, for one who dwells outside the Land is as though he has no Elohim, etc. (Ketubot 110b). And the withdrawal of the Shekhinah is joined to the ger-ship, for the one depends on the other, that the withdrawal of the Shekhinah follows from the ger-ship, that they would be as strangers outside the Land in a place of withdrawal of the Shekhinah. ״וַעֲבָדוּם״ — this is an additional thing beyond the ger-ship, for an ordinary stranger is not a slave to rulers. ״וְעִנּוּ אֹתָם״ — this is an additional thing beyond servitude, for an ordinary slave is not afflicted for nothing. And in the deliverance, the Holy One, blessed be He, saw fit to deliver them gradually, little by little. First He delivered them from the most dangerous thing, which is the affliction, and concerning this it is said ״וְהוֹצֵאתִי אֶתְכֶם מִתַּחַת סִבְלוֹת מִצְרַיִם״, for the burdens, that is the affliction, as it is written ״לְמַעַן עַנּוֹתוֹ בְּסִבְלֹתָם״ (Exodus 1:11). And afterwards He delivered them also from the servitude, and concerning this it is said ״וְהִצַּלְתִּי אֶתְכֶם מֵעֲבֹדָתָם״. And afterwards He delivered them from the lightest of them all, which is the ger-ship, and concerning this He said ״וְגָאַלְתִּי אֶתְכֶם״ etc., for an ordinary stranger has no redeemer, as it is written (Numbers 5:8) ״וְאִם אֵין לָאִישׁ גֹּאֵל״ — our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Bava Kamma 109a), this is the stranger, etc." "And since from the ger-ship there follows the withdrawal of the Shekhinah — for this reason it was necessary to join to the ger-ship the statement ״בְּאֶרֶץ לֹא לָהֶם״ (Genesis 15:13), which indicates the withdrawal of the Shekhinah — correspondingly He said here that with the nullification of the ger-ship they would merit the cleaving of the Shekhinah, and concerning this He said ״וְלָקַחְתִּי אֶתְכֶם לִי לְעָם וְהָיִיתִי לָכֶם לֵאלֹהִים״ (Exodus 6:7) — an actual taking, like the way of a man in the world. And since one who dwells outside the Land is as though he has no Elohim, therefore He said here ״וְהָיִיתִי לָכֶם לֵאלֹהִים״. And through this cleaving ״וִידַעְתֶּם כִּי אֲנִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם הַמּוֹצִיא אֶתְכֶם מִתַּחַת סִבְלוֹת מִצְרָיִם״ (Exodus 6:7) — the greatest of the evils. And afterwards He explained this taking by saying ״וְהֵבֵאתִי אֶתְכֶם אֶל הָאָרֶץ״ etc. (Exodus 6:8). And therefore they instituted at Passover four cups corresponding to the deliverance from these four evils. And above, in the portion of וַיֵּשֶׁב (Genesis 40:11), regarding the cup mentioned in the butler's dream, you will find a very sound reason for the four cups of Passover; see there." "And behold: in these four expressions of redemption, the fact that He mentioned the name Elohim four times in the verse (Exodus 3:15) ״כֹּה תֹאמַר אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֱלֹהֵי אֲבוֹתֵיכֶם אֱלֹהֵי אַבְרָהָם אֱלֹהֵי יִצְחָק וֵאלֹהֵי יַעֲקֹב שְׁלָחַנִי אֲלֵיכֶם״ — a general term and particulars, why do I need both? Rather, to say that from the ger-ship they were delivered in the merit of Abraham, who went forth from his land and from his birthplace and from his father's house to a foreign land; and in the merit of the service of Isaac, who was bound upon the altar for the service of HaShem, they were delivered from the servitude of Egypt; and in the merit of Jacob, who suffered afflictions all his days, they were delivered from the affliction of Egypt. But regarding the matter of divine cleaving, all the fathers were equal in it; therefore He gathered them together and said ״אֱלֹהֵי אֲבוֹתֵיכֶם״ — that in the merit of all of them they would merit the cleaving of the Shekhinah. And some say ״וְהִצַּלְתִּי אֶתְכֶם מֵעֲבֹדָתָם״ means from the constellation of the Ram, which the Egyptians worshipped, that is to say, from their divinity — which is עֲבוֹדָתָם, 'their service' — and this is correct."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {2 "The wordplay עֲבֹדָתָם ('their servitude' / 'their worship') rests on the dual meaning of עבודה as labor and as idolatrous worship; the traditional reading that the Egyptians worshipped the zodiacal sign of the Ram is found in Rashi on Exodus 12:2 and other sources."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:44.593487029Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}